## Mutation Analysis

The mutation removes a `__syncthreads()` call at line 70 in the original code. Looking at the original CUDA kernel `fused_gemm_bn_gelu_kernel`:

```cuda
for (int t = 0; t < (K + TILE_SIZE - 1) / TILE_SIZE; ++t) {
    int tiled_k = t * TILE_SIZE;

    // Load tile from input matrix A
    if (row < M && (tiled_k + threadIdx.x) < K) {
        tile_a[threadIdx.y][threadIdx.x] = input[row * K + tiled_k + threadIdx.x];
    } else {
        tile_a[threadIdx.y][threadIdx.x] = 0.0;
    }

    // Load tile from weight matrix B (transposed)
    if (col < N && (tiled_k + threadIdx.y) < K) {
        tile_b[threadIdx.y][threadIdx.x] = weight[col * K + tiled_k + threadIdx.y];
    } else {
        tile_b[threadIdx.y][threadIdx.x] = 0.0;
    }

    __syncthreads();  // First sync - ensures all tiles are loaded

    // Compute partial dot product
    for (int k = 0; k < TILE_SIZE; ++k) {
        acc += tile_a[threadIdx.y][k] * tile_b[k][threadIdx.x];
    }

    __syncthreads();  // Second sync (line 70) - REMOVED in mutant
}
```

The second `__syncthreads()` at line 70 is removed in the mutant. This sync is at the end of the loop iteration, before the next iteration starts.


The barrier at the end of each iteration prevents threads from overwriting shared memory tiles while other threads are still consuming data from them. Without it, a thread could begin loading new tile data before all threads finish their computations, causing some threads to read stale or partially-updated values. This introduces a data race condition where the computation reads corrupted intermediate values.

For the specific kernel dimensions: with `K = 512` and `TILE_SIZE = 32`, the loop executes 16 iterations. The thread block processes a `32 x 32` tile of the output matrix per iteration. The synchronization point prevents the next iteration's tile load from corrupting data that threads are still reading from the current iteration.

Without `__syncthreads()`, faster threads advance to the next iteration and overwrite shared memory while slower threads finish reading the current tile. This corrupts the accumulated results in unpredictable ways. The mutation is semantically distinguishable because the race produces incorrect GEMM outputs that propagate through BatchNorm and GELU, altering the final model predictions.

To expose this reliably, I should use large-magnitude inputs and weights so any corrupted tile reads produce visibly different accumulations. Non-uniform values make the corruption detectable rather than canceling out. Since there are 16 tile iterations and 1024 threads per block, thread divergence is guaranteed, making the race condition highly probable with the right input values.

For the test, I'll use tensors filled with consistent large values like 10.0—this makes the expected output predictable, so deviations from race-condition corruption stand out immediately. Random high-magnitude values would also work, but uniform values give a clearer baseline to detect anomalies against.

The key insight is that the race condition happens during GEMM operations with the model's weights. Since those weights are initialized with kaiming_uniform and have reasonable magnitudes, feeding large-magnitude inputs amplifies any numerical errors from unsynchronized memory access, making them exceed tolerance thresholds and trigger test failures. of work, making the race condition manifest in different ways across runs. The corruption from unsynchronized memory access will accumulate across iterations, pushing results well beyond any reasonable tolerance when compared to the sequential baseline.
